fail fast fail often. Of course not. Imagine being the head of a factory making Toyota automobiles who decides, let's fail fast today. No, no, no, no. So, rather than a blanket rejection of failure or a blanket embrace of failure, you say that we need to stop treating all mistakes as if they are the same. And you cite the social scientist, Sim Sittkin, who once made the case for something he called

