But unfortunately, Roka and his immediate colleagues were never able to get senior managers at NASA to take the problem seriously, to really believe that there was a problem. Was this partly because there had been other foam strikes that turned out to be fairly minimal routine maintenance kinds of issues? Exactly. So unfortunately, people at NASA had learned to equate the foam strikes that did happen with just maintenance. They're not a safety risk, but they had had so many of these little tiny foam strikes that they didn't think it was worth looking into. And in some ways this is sort of understandable, even though in retrospect we know this was a mistake,

